  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot \u0394x\n$$\n\nwhere $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+k\u0394x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\u0394x=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,\u27f9\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8{)}^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8{)}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8{)}^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$",
